  "weaknesses": [
    {
      "point": "The model assumes mechanically equilibrated, elastic-bias-free linkages but does not explicitly incorporate chemical potentials or quantify the free energy transduced from ATP hydrolysis. To strengthen the biophysical claims, the authors should demonstrate how the full reaction network satisfies global detailed balance and calculate thermodynamic efficiency (work output versus ΔG_ATP input) rather than relying solely on pathway incidence metrics.",
      "topic": "Thermodynamic consistency"
    },
    {
      "point": "Reaction rates are assigned based on logical hierarchies and arbitrary order-of-magnitude differences (e.g., k_uni as the speed limit, specific off-rate values to enforce binding competition) without empirical or physical derivation. The manuscript lacks a systematic sensitivity analysis to show how robust the target cycle operation is to parameter variations, making it unclear whether the observed cycling emerges from fundamental design principles or fine-tuned rate assignments.",
      "topic": "Rate parameterization"
    },
    {
      "point": "The authors explicitly state that dwell times and cycle times were not analyzed, focusing only on pathway incidence and turnover rates. This omission severely limits kinetic validation; to substantiate claims about timing, rate-limiting steps, and the role of catalysis in rectification, the simulations should report dwell time distributions for each reactant and mean first-passage times for complete cycles.",
      "topic": "Kinetic characterization"
    },
    {
      "point": "Cleavage is modeled as a topological split that introduces mechanical flexibility, equating this directly to chemical hydrolysis without bridging the abstraction to established kinetic frameworks like transition state theory or activation barriers. The authors should clarify how the arbitrary cleavage and ligation rates map to physical energy landscapes and justify why this topological change adequately captures the kinetics of bond breaking and forming in real ATPases.",
      "topic": "Catalysis abstraction"
    },
    {
      "point": "The efficiency metric (E = v_target / (v_target + v_futile)) measures pathway fidelity but does not account for thermodynamic or mechanical work output. In biophysical contexts, efficiency typically relates to useful work performed per cycle relative to the chemical potential driving force. The authors should either redefine efficiency in thermodynamic terms or explicitly acknowledge that this metric only reflects topological pathway selection rather than functional energy transduction.",
      "topic": "Efficiency definition"
    }
  ]
}
